endobj
403 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (The Refugee Act: Legal Framework Governing Refugees in Malawi)
/E (The Refugee Act: Legal Framework Governing Refugees in Malawi)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
404 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (06)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
405 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
406 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The Act provides the legal foundation for the establishment of refugee camps and the regulation of refugee status determination procedures. It mandates that refugees live in designated camps and outlines their rights and obligations within these spaces. However, the Act also imposes significant restrictions on refugees, particularly in terms of movement, employment, and access to basic services, which have sparked debates about its respect of human rights standards.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
407 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T <4D616C6177699073205265736572766174696F6E7320746F20746865203139353120554E205265667567656520436F6E76656E74696F6E>
/E <4D616C6177699073205265736572766174696F6E7320746F20746865203139353120554E205265667567656520436F6E76656E74696F6E>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
408 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (When Malawi ratified the 1951 Refugee Convention, it made specific reservations that limit certain rights of refugees. These reservations include:)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
409 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1043 0 R
/ActualText (Freedom of Movement: Refugees are required to reside in designated camps and cannot move freely within the country without special permission.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
410 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1044 0 R
/ActualText (Employment: Refugees are restricted from engaging in employment or self-employment outside of the camps.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
411 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1045 0 R
/ActualText (Public Relief and Assistance: Access to social services such as education, healthcare, and social security is limited for refugees.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
412 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
413 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Unfulfilled Government Pledges)
/E (Unfulfilled Government Pledges)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
414 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Over the years, the Malawi government has made multiple pledges to improve conditions for refugees, pledging to revise the 1989 Refugee Act and to lift specific reservations to the 1951 Refugee Convention. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
415 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (To date, none of these promises have been fulfilled, leaving refugees in Malawi in persistent hardship, unable to fully access their rights or improve their living conditions. In 2018, Malawi signed to adopt the Comprehensive Refugee Response Framework \(CRRF\) under the New York Declaration endorsed by 193 UN member states in 2016. The CRRF aims to integrate refugees into host communities, promote their human rights, and reduce dependency on aid, but so far, Malawi has not implemented any of these measures.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
