588 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Human trafficking in Dzaleka Refugee Camp is part of a well-organized chain that begins in Ethiopia, where young people are deceived with promises of a better life in South Africa. Traffickers in Dzaleka - often Ethiopian nationals posing as asylum seekers - serve as key facilitators of this network, and are among the most powerful in the operation. )
/E (Human trafficking in Dzaleka Refugee Camp is part of a well-organized chain that begins in Ethiopia, where young people are deceived with promises of a better life in South Africa. Traffickers in Dzaleka - often Ethiopian nationals posing as asylum seekers - serve as key facilitators of this network, and are among the most powerful in the operation. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
589 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (In Ethiopia, travellers embark on a journey firstly receiving assistance from network members in Kenya before being transported through Tanzania into Malawi. Close to the Malawian border, dedicated transporters with connections to local police and immigration authorities generally facilitate a smooth transit into Malawi, allowing most travellers to reach Dzaleka Refugee Camp with minimal interruption. However, sources close to the traffickers indicate that disruptions along the way often arise from internal conflicts within the network. Conspiracies and power struggles among members can lead to intentional arrests of rivals, causing sporadic detainments and setbacks for those in transit. )
/E (In Ethiopia, travellers embark on a journey firstly receiving assistance from network members in Kenya before being transported through Tanzania into Malawi. Close to the Malawian border, dedicated transporters with connections to local police and immigration authorities generally facilitate a smooth transit into Malawi, allowing most travellers to reach Dzaleka Refugee Camp with minimal interruption. However, sources close to the traffickers indicate that disruptions along the way often arise from internal conflicts within the network. Conspiracies and power struggles among members can lead to intentional arrests of rivals, causing sporadic detainments and setbacks for those in transit. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
590 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (In Dzaleka, traffickers hold their victims hostage in hidden "prisons" within the camp. There, captives face torture, starvation, and even death if their families fail to pay hefty ransoms. )
/E (In Dzaleka, traffickers hold their victims hostage in hidden "prisons" within the camp. There, captives face torture, starvation, and even death if their families fail to pay hefty ransoms. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
591 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Holding conditions are horrific, with minimal food and no proper medical care; victims who die are secretly buried by refugee youth hired by the traffickers. Despite the fact that these operations are well known to both the refugees and camp authorities, little has been done to stop them. Rival trafficking gangs sometimes arrange for the arrest or deportation of competitors, further victimizing those caught in the web of exploitation.)
/E (Holding conditions are horrific, with minimal food and no proper medical care; victims who die are secretly buried by refugee youth hired by the traffickers. Despite the fact that these operations are well known to both the refugees and camp authorities, little has been done to stop them. Rival trafficking gangs sometimes arrange for the arrest or deportation of competitors, further victimizing those caught in the web of exploitation.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
